oever followed them and correctly did what they told them, that those people are going to paradise. Where is the evidence, okay, for these understandings? It's the verse itself, because the verse says conditions (alBaqarah2:62). It says: believed in Allah. Who is Allah? Allah, His description is given in the Qur'an. You go to the Qur'an, chapter:112 of the Qur'an. It says, say, Allah is uniquely one. Allah is independent.", "Allah does not give birth, nor is born. So He doesn't have children. He's not a father of anyone, and there is nothing like unto Him. So whoever believes in Allah, Christians of today don't believe in Allah. Christians of today believe in the Trinity, and they believe that God, the Father, God, the Son, God, the Holy Spirit, and this, they don't believe in Allah, in that definition of Allah that is given in the Qur'an. They don't believe in that. And they also don't do righteous deeds, because the righteous deeds are that which is prescribed in the Qur'an.", "They can do some righteous deeds, yes, like giving charity or this or that, but the main righteous deeds, which are like the five pillars of Islam, for example, these are the main righteous deeds. They don't do them. They don't give the زكاه (zakat), obligation of the zakat (charity) They don't fast Ramadan. They don't pray five times a day. So that what qualifies the verse and makes you understand that this is referring to Muslims or the people who followed in the past, the religion of their prophets is that when the verse says: they believe in Allah and they do righteous deeds. So that's how we understand this verse. Of course, this verse cannot be referring to that. Does that make sense?",))
